// @error return type mismatch: expected i32, got i32 | void
// ORACLE PIN: this records WHICH LAYER owns a void if-expression arm. VL types the arms of
// an if-expression and joins them, a void arm included — the join here is `i32 | void` — and
// rules a `void` member in a value slot a TYPE error. That is the language's answer, and it
// is the CHECKER's: the same rule fires for `const x = sideeffect()` ("cannot bind the void
// result"), for `take(sideeffect())` ("argument 1: expected i32, got void"), for
// `xs.push(sideeffect())`, for `sideeffect() + 1`, and for an annotated `return`, as here.
//
// The rule is applied on the ANNOTATED paths only. With the annotation removed this same
// body reaches the EMITTER instead and is rejected there
// (`error-if-arm-ends-in-void-call.vl`) — so the emit reject is a backstop for a checker
// hole on the INFERENCE path, not a separate ruling. Keeping both pins side by side is what
// makes the hole visible: two files, one shape, two layers, and the annotation is the only
// difference between them.
function sideeffect() { print(9) }
function f(c: boolean): i32 { return if c { 1 } else { sideeffect() } }
print(f(true))
